1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,900
Raja Ravi Varma painted the Indian epics in oil.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,400
What if he could paint any scene you describe?

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:11,900
This is The Next Ravi Varma, by Team The Unsupervised.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,900
You type a scene from the Mahabharata or Ramayana,

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,900
optionally add a photo of someone posing,

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,900
and get a new painting in his oil style.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,500
Our project follows the four milestones of our proposal:

8
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:31,900
the data, the style, the words, and the pose, all inside one web app.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,900
Milestone one: the data. From one hundred and thirty-five images,

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:44,900
we kept only his sixty-one oil paintings, not the flat printed copies, and held nine out for testing.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,900
Qwen three V L captioned every painting,

12
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,900
and each caption starts with our trigger phrase: R R Varma oil painting.

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,900
Milestone two: the style. We trained a three-megabyte Lora on frozen Stable Diffusion one point five.

14
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,900
Plain Stable Diffusion paints a photo, and asking for Ravi Varma gives a calendar print.

15
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:15,900
Our Lora paints in oil: style similarity zero point eight two, close to zero point eight four for his real paintings.

16
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,900
Milestone three: the words. Qwen two point five expands a scene into a full prompt.

17
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,900
A clip judge then picks the best of four paintings,

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,900
raising scene match from twenty-eight to thirty.

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,900
Milestone four: the pose. Open Pose turns a photo into a skeleton,

20
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,400
and Control Net paints a figure that follows it,

21
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:43,900
with character looks, pose checks and a high-resolution refine.

22
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,900
Text alone gets poses wrong and adds extra people.

23
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,900
With a pose photo, pose accuracy jumps from ten to ninety-seven percent,

24
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,900
with no loss in scene match.

25
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:01,900
Here is our web app. I choose a scene and press Paint.

26
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,900
Four paintings are made and the best is kept; the prompt and seed are shown below.

27
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:17,900
Fast mode gives a quick single-image preview.

28
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,900
Now I upload a photo of someone drawing a bow and press Paint.

29
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,900
The app shows the skeleton and paints Chitrangada in exactly that pose.

30
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,900
All of this runs on a free Colab T four GPU.

31
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,900
His style, any scene, any pose.

32
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,900
Our code, data and evaluations are public on GitHub. Thank you.

